  "Question": "If \\(\\angle A\\) is four times \\(\\angle B,\\) and the complement of \\(\\angle B\\) is four times the complement of \\(\\angle A,\\) then \\(\\angle B=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10^\\circ\\)",
    "B": "\\(12^\\circ\\)",
    "C": "\\(15^\\circ\\)",
    "D": "\\(18^\\circ\\)",
    "E": "\\(22.5^\\circ\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "A complement has measure \\(90^\\circ\\) minus the original angle, so expressing both angles using one unknown gives a single equation. Let \\(x\\) be the degree measure of \\(\\angle B\\). Then \\(\\angle A\\) has degree measure \\(4x\\), and the complement condition becomes\n\\[\n90-x=4(90-4x).\n\\]\nExpanding and collecting terms gives\n\\[\n90-x=360-16x,\n\\qquad 15x=270,\n\\qquad x=18.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\angle A=72^\\circ\\). Their complements are \\(72^\\circ\\) and \\(18^\\circ\\), respectively, so both stated four-to-one relationships hold. Therefore \\(\\boxed{\\angle B=18^\\circ}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
